course: dictionary
lesson: 12495
title: Denounce: saying something is wrong
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: To denounce something is to say, clearly and strongly, that it is wrong or bad. Often you say it out loud, in front of other people. «The head teacher denounced cheating in the test.» The sentence tells you she was not happy about the cheating, and she said so firmly.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. The meaning uses wrong, bad and evil, so denounce is a strong word. It is more than saying "I don't like that". It is saying it firmly and publicly.
explain: Denounce can also mean telling the authorities about someone who has done wrong. «She denounced the thief to the police.» Here it is not just an opinion: a person has reported a wrongdoer so they can be dealt with.
fact: Denounce means to say publicly and strongly that something or someone is wrong, bad or evil.
fact: «Denounce» is a verb: denounce, denounces, denounced, denouncing.
fact: «Denounce as» is a useful phrase: «The city denounced the plan as unfair.»
fact: «Denounce someone to the authorities» means to report them so they can be punished.
fact: Leaders often denounce violence or cruelty, and it can take courage to denounce bullying.
exercise: «The mayor denounced the attack as cruel and wrong.» What does denounced mean here? | Gave a prize for it | Whispered it quietly to a friend | *Said publicly that it was wrong | Forgot all about it
exercise: «Sam saw Tom cheating, so he denounced him to the teacher.» What did Sam do? | Hid behind the door | *Reported Tom because he had done wrong | Said sorry for Tom | Copied Tom's answers
exercise: Which word means to say strongly, in public, that something is wrong? | Dance | Doze | Donate | *Denounce
test: The newspaper denounced the unfair law. What did the newspaper do? | *Said publicly that the law was wrong | Forgot to mention it | Changed the law itself | Praised it loudly
test: Why might someone choose to denounce bullying? | To get a bigger lunch | To make friends with the bully | *To show it is wrong and to help stop it | Because it is a fun game
exam: Which form of denounce is the past tense? | *Denounced | Denouncing | Denounces | Denounce
